#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.13"
# dependencies = ["httpx2"]
# ///
"""Watch the GitHub Actions runs on every repo in the registry, redrawn in place.

    watch-ci [-n SECONDS] [-k RUNS] [--repo NAME]... [--once]
    watch-ci --commit REF --wait [--repo NAME] [-n SECONDS] [--start-within SECONDS] [--timeout SECONDS] [--json]

A repo's run list is read every interval while the repo is live: a run of it is
unfinished, or one finished in the last HOT_FOR seconds. Every other repo is read
once each IDLE_EVERY seconds, the repos spread across that time, so a run starting
there shows within IDLE_EVERY. GitHub's secondary limit allows 900 REST points a
minute and 100 requests at once, and its docs do not say whether a 304 counts. So
the idle cadence keeps a registry of a hundred repos under 900 a minute either way,
and every REST request waits for one of IN_FLIGHT_MOST slots. The live GraphQL
query has its own lane, so a burst of REST reads never delays it.

The screen redraws every interval from whatever has landed, and at once when the
terminal is resized. Fetching runs beside it, so a slow poll delays the data but
never the clock on the screen.

Each run says what it ran for: the trigger, the pull request it tested or merged,
the tag on its commit, and who started it when that was not the owner. The pull
request comes from the repo's pull request list rather than the run's own
`pull_requests`, because GitHub empties that once the pull request closes. A push
run is matched to the pull request whose merge commit it ran on.

The board is one table under a row of column names, the shape of `gh run list`.
Each run is one row: its age beside its symbol, its repo, its title, how long it
took, its workflow and number, and last how a failure resolved, its trigger and
its tag. Each job is a row below it: its step beside its symbol, then on the
run's columns how long it took, its name, and its runner or attempts. Each column
is as wide as its widest cell while the screen has room, measured again on every
frame. No row wraps. Past the screen's width the trigger column gives way first,
then the run column, then the title, and text cut short ends in an ellipsis.

Under each running or queued run is a row per job: how long it has run, its
runner, and its current step with how long that step has run, or the labels a
waiting job needs. A finished job says how it ended, how long ago, and how many
steps it ran. A job's time takes its symbol's color, so a passed job's is
green beside its check. A running run's TOOK is its time so far over the median of
its workflow's last successful runs, red once past twice that median. A queued
run's AGE is how long it has waited. A run is RUNNING once any job of its attempt has started, and
QUEUED until then, because GitHub calls a whole run queued while any one of its
jobs waits for a runner.

FAILED lists every run with a failed job in any attempt, and under it one row per
failed job: its newest failed attempt, and how many attempts failed. A run's
conclusion is its latest attempt's, so a run whose failed job was rerun, or that
was cancelled after a job failed, would otherwise hide that failure. A failure
whose rerun passed, whose workflow has passed on that branch since, or whose pull
request has closed says so in green and drops its log excerpt. A standing one
shows its age in red, and under its step the lines of the step's log that name
the failure, read once per job.

Every GET sends If-None-Match with the last ETag, and GitHub does not count a 304
against the hourly rate limit, so reading a repo's runs is free against it while
nothing changes. A 304 touches the cached body, so its mtime says when GitHub last
confirmed it.

A running run's jobs change on every step, so a REST read of them is almost
never a 304. Every live run's jobs and steps instead come from one GraphQL query,
sent again as soon as it answers but never more than once a second while a job
is running. While none is, as when every live job waits for a runner, it is sent
every LIVE_QUIET_EVERY seconds, or at once when a run list changes. GraphQL has
no 304, so each query costs a point of its own 5,000-point hour, apart from the
REST limit. Steps are asked for only on jobs the last answer had running, which
keeps the query at one point until about a hundred jobs and runs are live. A
bigger query is slower to answer, so many live runs refresh less often rather
than costing more. The REST jobs carry the runner and the labels a queued job
needs, which GraphQL's check runs lack, so a live run's REST jobs are read when
the live view shows a job the REST copy has not seen start or finish. Any other
run that is not a first-attempt success is read once after its last update. Where
the live query fails, live runs' REST jobs are read every JOBS_EVERY.

A repo's pull requests are read when a run newer than the last read appears, and
its tags when a run finishes, because a pull request list changes on every
comment and push. The leader publishes when it last read each, so a new leader
does not read them all again. A spent hourly limit holds the API that refused it, REST or
GraphQL, until its reset. Any other 403 or 429 that names a rate limit is the
secondary limit, which REST and GraphQL share, so it holds both. It holds for the
`retry-after` GitHub sends. Where it sends none, the hold starts at HOLD_FIRST
seconds and doubles with each refusal in a row, up to HOLD_MOST.

Every instance watching the same repos shares one poll. The first to start takes a
lock in the cache directory and polls. Every other instance draws from the cache,
and from the live view, budgets and errors the leader publishes beside it. Inside
tmux an instance is watching only while its window is on screen, and the leader
polls while any instance is watching. When the leader exits the kernel frees the
lock, and the next instance to try takes over.

The screen fits the terminal's height. Running and queued runs are placed first,
then standing failures, then the other finished runs newest first, so a long
FAILED list never pushes a live run off the top.

`--commit REF --wait` draws no board. It blocks on one commit's runs and says what
became of them, and a session starts it in the background:

    watch-ci --commit HEAD --wait --json

The repo is `--repo`, or else the one this directory's origin remote names. The ref
is resolved by git where this directory's origin is that repo, so HEAD and a short
sha mean what the session means. GitHub resolves what git cannot. The commit's run
list is read every COMMIT_INTERVAL seconds, because the board's one second would
spend the account's shared REST pool. It ends once every run seen has completed and
a read at least one interval after the last of them finished finds no new run,
because a push can start a second workflow a little later. A commit whose runs
finished longer ago than that has had its interval already, so it ends on the first
read.

Exit 0 means every run concluded success, skipped or neutral, and 1 means one
concluded anything else. Exit 2 is a usage error or a ref naming no commit. Exit 3 is
no verdict: no run within --start-within, or runs still going at --timeout. Exit 4 is
no answer from the API: never reached, timed out, or a 429 or 5xx, on all
READ_ATTEMPTS attempts. A refusal also exits 1, and its line on stderr says so.
A commit lookup or a first read takes that exit 4 at once. Once a read has answered,
the run list keeps being read for NO_ANSWER_MOST seconds, so a blip does not cost the
wait. Exit 4 then names the runs still going and does not call them failed. A rate
limit holds the reads until its hold ends. A wait cut off while no read answers exits
4, never 3, because no read has said the runs are absent or unfinished.

stdout is one line per run: conclusion, workflow, run id, url. Under a failed run sits
each failed job's name and the log lines naming its failure. `--json` prints one
object instead: {repo, ref, sha, conclusion, reason, waited_seconds, runs: [{id,
workflow, status, conclusion, url, event, head_branch, run_attempt, created_at,
run_started_at, updated_at, failed_jobs: [{id, name, url, failed_step, excerpt}]}]}.
The top conclusion is success, failure, no_run_started, timed_out, no_answer or
refused. stderr says when a run changes state, and why no verdict came.
"""

